The Fisher Sensitivity Score is a pruning metric for 3D Gaussian primitives that estimates the impact of each Gaussian on the L2 reconstruction loss using a block-diagonal approximation of the Hessian. It sets itself apart from visibility-based siblings like Efficient Pruning Score by evaluating geometric sensitivity through the log-determinant of parameter gradients, thereby avoiding the bias toward larger Gaussians inherent in pixel-coverage heuristics. Inputs and Outputs: Input: A trained 3D Gaussian scene model (means \mathbf{x}i, scales \mathbf{s}i, etc.) and a set of training views. Output: A scalar sensitivity score Ui for each Gaussian i, used to rank and prune primitives. Data Structures: The method operates on the standard 3D Gaussian attributes. For the score calculation, only the spatial mean \mathbf{x}i \in \mathbb{R}^3 and scaling parameters \mathbf{s}i \in \mathbb{R}^3 are utilized. Formulas: The base loss is the L2 error over ground truth pixels \mathcal{P}{gt}: L2 =  rac{1}{2} \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} \|I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}\|2^2 \quad (5) The full Hessian of this loss with respect to all Gaussian parameters \mathcal{G} is: 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 L2 = \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T + (I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}) 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) \quad (6) On a converged model, the residual term (I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}) approaches zero, causing the second-order term to vanish. The Hessian approximates the Fisher Information Matrix: 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 L2 \approx \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T \quad (7) The sensitivity score Ui for Gaussian i is defined as the log-determinant of the block-diagonal approximation restricted to the mean \mathbf{x}i and scale \mathbf{s}i parameters: Ui = \log |
abla{\mathbf{x}i, \mathbf{s}i} I{\mathcal{G}} 
abla{\mathbf{x}i, \mathbf{s}i} I{\mathcal{G}}^T| \quad (10) The authors argue that mean and scale capture the projective geometric invariances between the 3D scene and input views, whereas rotation parameters are excluded as they do not induce geometric change under these invariances. Steps: 1. Patch Selection: Render the scene at a lower resolution to form image patches. The standard patch size is 4 	imes 4 pixels. 2. Gradient Computation: For each pixel in the patches, compute the gradient of the rendered image I{\mathcal{G}} with respect to the mean \mathbf{x}i and scale \mathbf{s}i of all visible Gaussians. 3. Fisher Approximation: Compute the outer product 
abla{\mathbf{x}i, \mathbf{s}i} I{\mathcal{G}} 
abla{\mathbf{x}i, \mathbf{s}i} I{\mathcal{G}}^T for each pixel. 4. Aggregation: Sum the Fisher approximations over all pixels within each patch and then sum these patch-wise results over all training views to obtain the scene-level Hessian approximation for each Gaussian. 5. Score Calculation: Compute Ui = \log |	ext{Hessian}{i}| for each Gaussian. 6. Pruning: Sort Gaussians by Ui and remove the least sensitive ones. 7. Refinement: Fine-tune the remaining model without densification for a fixed number of iterations (e.g., 5,000) to recover quality. 8. Iteration: Repeat steps 1-7 for multiple rounds (e.g., two rounds) to achieve higher compression ratios. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def computefisherscore(gaussians, trainingviews, patchsize=4): """ Computes the Fisher Sensitivity Score for each Gaussian. gaussians: List of Gaussian objects with attributes.mean (3,),.scale (3,) trainingviews: List of view objects with.image (H, W, 3),.cameraparams patchsize: int, dimension of the square patch (default 4) """ n = len(gaussians) k = 6 # 3 mean + 3 scale parameters Haccum = [[0.0] (k k) for in range(n)] for view in trainingviews: # Render scene at reduced resolution; each low-res pixel aggregates a patchsize x patchsize region lowresimage = renderdownsampled(view, gaussians, factor=patchsize) Himg, Wimg, = lowresimage.shape for y in range(Himg): for x in range(Wimg): # Compute gradients of the rendered pixel color w.r.t. mean and scale for all visible Gaussians # Using automatic differentiation through the differentiable splatting kernel grads = computegradients(view, gaussians, x, y, patchsize) # grads[i] is a list of k floats: [dI/dxi, dI/dyi, dI/dzi, dI/sxi, dI/syi, dI/szi] for i in range(n): g = grads[i] # Accumulate outer product for the block-diagonal Hessian approximation for r in range(k): for c in range(k): Haccum[i][r k + c] += g[r] g[c] scores = [0.0] n for i in range(n): # Extract k x k matrix from flattened accumulator Hmat = [[Haccum[i][r k + c] for c in range(k)] for r in range(k)] detval = computedeterminant(Hmat) # Log-determinant score; add epsilon to handle singular matrices scores[i] = math.log(max(abs(detval), 1e-8)) return scores def renderdownsampled(view, gaussians, factor): # Standard differentiable rasterization at reduced resolution return render(view, gaussians, resolutionscale=1.0 / factor) def computegradients(view, gaussians, x, y, patchsize): # Standard automatic differentiation through the splatting kernel # Returns gradient of pixel color w.r.t. mean and scale for each Gaussian return backproppixel(view, gaussians, x, y, patchsize) Parameters: Patch Size: 4 	imes 4 pixels. Pruning Percentages: 80% in the first round, 50% of the remaining Gaussians in the second round (total 90% compression). Fine-tuning Iterations: 5,000 iterations per round. Edge Cases and Pitfalls: Singular Hessians: If the Hessian approximation is singular, the log-determinant is undefined. Regularization or absolute value handling is required. Visibility Bias: Unlike visibility-based scores, this method does not inherently bias toward larger Gaussians, which is a common pitfall in methods like LightGaussian. Convergence Requirement: The approximation relies on the model being converged; otherwise, the residual term in the Hessian is significant. A strategy where the prune-refine cycle is repeated. It allows the model to recover quality after aggressive pruning, enabling higher compression ratios (e.g., 90%) than single-round methods. The conceptual basis for the score, linking the log-determinant to the uncertainty of the Gaussian's contribution to the image plane geometry. Time Complexity: Computing the full Hessian is expensive as it requires summing over all pixels/views. The patch-wise approximation reduces this cost by rendering at lower resolution. Memory Complexity: Requires storing a 6 	imes 6 accumulator per Gaussian, resulting in O(N) memory where N is the number of Gaussians. Performance: On Mip-NeRF 360, PUP 3D-GS achieves 26.67 PSNR, 0.7862 SSIM, 0.2719 LPIPS, 204.81 FPS, and 74.65 MB size after 90% pruning. It increases rendering speed by 3.56x on average compared to LightGaussian. Strengths: Retains smaller Gaussians better than visibility-based methods; principled mathematical foundation. Limitations: Computationally intensive during the pruning phase; requires multiple rounds for optimal compression. The method was introduced in "PUP 3D-GS: Principled Uncertainty Pruning for 3D Gaussian Splatting" (2025). It builds on the Fisher Information Matrix concept from statistical estimation theory, applying it to the differentiable rendering pipeline of 3D Gaussian Splatting. The method was developed to address the limitations of heuristic pruning scores, such as the bias toward large Gaussians in LightGaussian. PUP 3D-GS: Principled Uncertainty Pruning for 3D Gaussian Splatting, https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52734.2025.00558, fetched 2026-10-29 Budgeted-GS: Real-Time Large-Scale Gaussian Splatting via Factoring LOD, https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03162, fetched 2026-10-29 (unofficial)

3D Student's t Distribution Splatting (SSS) is a neural rendering method that replaces the 3D Gaussian primitives of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) with flexible Student's t distributions and extends the splatting scheme to include both positive (splatting) and negative (scooping) density components. It solves the expressivity limitations of monotonic Gaussian mixtures by allowing fat tails and non-monotonic density modeling, achieving higher parameter efficiency (reducing component counts by up to 82%) while maintaining or improving rendering quality. Unlike 3DGS, SSS requires Stochastic Gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (SGHMC) for optimization to prevent mode collapse in the learnable degrees of freedom parameter 
u. Inputs: A set of 3D primitives, each defined by a mean position  oldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathbb{R}^3, a covariance matrix \Sigma \in \mathbb{R}^{3	imes3}, a degree of freedom 
u \in [1, 10000], opacity o \in [0, 1], and color coefficients c \in \mathbb{R}^{27} (Spherical Harmonics). The covariance is parameterized as \Sigma = R S S^T R^T where R is rotation and S is scaling. Outputs: A 2D image where each pixel color C(u) is computed via alpha blending of projected 2D Student's t distributions. Data Structures: Each primitive i stores:  oldsymbol{\mu}i: 3D mean (vector). Ri, Si: Rotation matrix and scaling matrix (factorized covariance). 
ui: Scalar degree of freedom. oi: Opacity scalar. ci: Color coefficients vector. wi: Weight scalar (derived from oi and compositing, initially positive). Rendering Steps: 1. Ray Casting: For each pixel u, cast a ray into the scene. 2. Primitive Selection: Identify all primitives i intersecting the ray. 3. Projection: For each primitive, compute the 2D projected mean  oldsymbol{\mu}^{2D} and covariance \Sigma^{2D}: Compute intermediate matrices: A = JW and b =  oldsymbol{x} + J( oldsymbol{d} -  oldsymbol{t}), where W is the affine view transformation, J is the approximate projective Jacobian,  oldsymbol{t} is camera coordinates, and  oldsymbol{x},  oldsymbol{d} are related to the ray origin and direction. Compute projected mean:  oldsymbol{\mu}^{2D} = (W oldsymbol{\mu} +  oldsymbol{t}){1:2} / ((W oldsymbol{\mu} +  oldsymbol{t})3). Compute projected covariance: \Sigma^{2D} = (JW\Sigma W^T J^T){1:2,1:2}. Note: The normalization constant of the 3D distribution is dropped to facilitate learning. 4. 2D Density Evaluation: Evaluate the 2D Student's t distribution Ti^{2D}(u) at pixel u: Ti^{2D}(u) = \left[1 +  rac{1}{
ui}(u -  oldsymbol{\mu}^{2D})^T(\Sigma^{2D})^{-1}(u -  oldsymbol{\mu}^{2D})ight]^{- rac{
ui+2}{2}}. 5. Alpha Blending: Composite colors using the standard alpha blending equation, adapted for the t-distribution density: C(u) = \sum{i=1}^N ci oi Ti^{2D}(u) \prod{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - oj Tj^{2D}(u)). Here, N is the number of intersecting primitives sorted by depth. Optimization: SGHMC: Vanilla SGD causes 
u to collapse to local minima (typically near 1). SSS uses Stochastic Gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (SGHMC) to decouple parameters and explore the 
u space effectively. Negative Components (Scooping): SSS introduces negative components to allow subtracting density, improving expressivity. The mixture model is non-monotonic. While a squared form P(x) = (\sum wi Ti(x))^2 was considered, it introduces pairwise interactions. The final implementation uses positive and negative components with a linear combination approach implied by the "splatting and scooping" description, though the exact final rendering equation for the non-monotonic mixture is not fully detailed in the provided excerpt beyond the standard alpha blending with potentially negative weights or densities. Low-Pass Filter: Unlike 3DGS, SSS does not use an analytical low-pass filter for minimum scale because the convolution of two Student's t distributions lacks an analytical form. For settings using only SGD with positive t-distributions, a small value (0.3) is added to the projected covariance matrix to prevent excessive tiny components. Gradients: The backward pass replaces Gaussian derivatives with t-distribution derivatives via the chain rule. Define helper function: h(x) = (x -  oldsymbol{\mu}')^T (\Sigma')^{-1} (x -  oldsymbol{\mu}') where  oldsymbol{\mu}' and \Sigma' are the projected 2D mean and covariance. Gradient w.r.t h(x):  rac{\partial T^{2D}}{\partial h(x)} = - rac{
u + 2}{2} \cdot  rac{1}{
u} \cdot \left[1 +  rac{1}{
u} h(x)ight]^{- rac{
u+4}{2}}. Gradient w.r.t 
u:  rac{\partial T^{2D}}{\partial 
u} =  rac{
u+2}{2} \cdot  rac{h(x)}{
u^2} \left[ 1 +  rac{1}{
u} h(x) ight]^{- rac{
u+4}{2}}. Gradients w.r.t  oldsymbol{\mu} and \Sigma follow the chain rule through h(x) and the projection transformations, similar to 3DGS but using the t-distribution derivatives. Component Recycling: When relocating low-opacity components to high-opacity ones, distribution invariance is maintained. For opacity, the relation is (1 - O{new})^N = (1 - O{old}). A hybrid optimization scheme for training Student's t-splatting that uses SGHMC as the base sampler but injects Adam's adaptive gradient scaling. It differs from vanilla 3DGS by replacing standard SGD with SGHMC+Adam, and from 3DGS-MCMC by incorporating Adam's gradient scaling and supporting negative components. Hyperparameters are largely identical to original 3DGS and 3DGS-MCMC, with new parameters introduced due to the Student's t distribution, negative components, and SGHMC integration. Time Complexity: Rendering is real-time (>70fps) on an NVIDIA RTX 4090, but slower than vanilla 3DGS due to the more complex t-distribution evaluation and SGHMC sampling overhead. Memory Complexity: Parameter efficiency is significantly higher; SSS can achieve comparable results to 3DGS while reducing the number of components by up to 82%. Strengths: Higher expressivity due to fat tails (covering larger areas with higher densities) and non-monotonic modeling (scooping). Better quality with fewer components. Limitations: Slower training and rendering than 3DGS. SGHMC sampling is slower than vanilla SGD. Requires careful handling of 
u to prevent collapse. When to use: When parameter efficiency and high expressivity are prioritized over raw training speed, or when Gaussian primitives fail to capture complex details with fat tails. SSS was proposed as an improvement on the fundamental paradigm of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS). It addresses the limitations of monotonic Gaussian mixture models by introducing flexible Student's t distributions and negative density components. The method was evaluated on Mip-NeRF 360, Tanks & Temples, and Deep Blending datasets, demonstrating state-of-the-art quality and parameter efficiency. The initial 3DGS method was presented as a neural rendering technique. 3D Student Splatting and Scooping, https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr52734.2025.01960, fetched 2026-10-29

The 4D Scale-adaptive Filter is a filtering technique for 4D Gaussian Splatting that prevents the distortion of Gaussian anisotropy and eliminates the rendering of invisible primitives during dynamic scene reconstruction. It distinguishes itself from fixed-scale smoothing filters by adaptively adjusting the dilation coefficient based on the primitive's current scale and visibility, thereby preserving high-frequency details while avoiding aliasing artifacts caused by over-dilation. The method modifies the covariance matrix of 4D Gaussian primitives during the splatting pipeline. It operates by computing an adaptive dilation scale \sigma{	ext{adapt}} that depends on the primitive's intrinsic scale, a minimum dilation ratio, and a visibility threshold. Inputs: \mathbf{G}k: The 4D Gaussian primitive k, defined by position \mathbf{p}k, covariance \mathbf{\Sigma}k(t), opacity, and color. st: The current scale of the Gaussian in a specific dimension at time t. \hat{
u}k: The estimated maximum sampling frequency for primitive k. \sigmas: A base scale factor. ho{\min}, ho{\max}: Minimum and maximum dilation ratios. ho{	ext{thre}}: Threshold for scale-based dilation gating. \epsilon: Minimal dilation scale for invisible Gaussians. Outputs: Filtered covariance \mathbf{\Sigma}'k(t) and amplitude scaling factor. Visibility mask M determining if the Gaussian contributes to the image. The 4D Gaussian primitive k maintains: \mathbf{p}k \in \mathbb{R}^3: Position. \mathbf{\Sigma}k(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{4 	imes 4}: Covariance matrix in 4D space (3D space + time). \hat{
u}k \in \mathbb{R}: Estimated maximum sampling frequency. st \in \mathbb{R}: Current scale magnitude (derived from \mathbf{\Sigma}k). ho{\min}: Minimum dilation ratio. Controls the lower bound of adaptive scaling. In ablation studies, a smaller ho{\min} is used to mitigate negative impacts on full-resolution reconstruction. \sigmas: Base scale factor. The minimal adaptive scale is computed as ho{\min} \cdot \sigmas. ho{\max}: Maximum dilation ratio. Constrains the expansion of enlarged dimensions to preserve anisotropy. ho{	ext{thre}}: Critical scale threshold. Determines when dilation is prevented and scale loss constraints are applied. Sensitivity to this hyperparameter affects lighting sensitivity. \epsilon: Minimal dilation scale for invisible primitives. Prevents imperceptible Gaussians from becoming visible due to dilation. 1. Estimate Sampling Frequency: Compute the rough minimum sampling interval \hat{T}k using initial positions \mathbf{p}k and camera parameters fn, dn: \hat{T}k = \min{n \in \{1, \dots, N\}} \left(  rac{1}{fn} \cdot  rac{dn}{\mathbf{1}n(\mathbf{p}k)} ight) Once the deformation field stabilizes, update the sampling frequency estimate \hat{
u}k using a momentum-based method. The maximum sampling frequency is determined by minimizing the ratio of distance to frequency across all cameras N: \hat{
u}k = \min{n \in \{1, \dots, N\}} \left(  rac{1}{fn(t)} \cdot  rac{dn(t)}{\mathbf{1}n(\mathbf{p}k(t))} ight) where \mathbf{1}n(\mathbf{p}k) is an indicator function (1 if visible, 0 otherwise). 2. Compute Adaptive Dilation Ratio: Calculate the proportional change in the dilation coefficient ho{	ext{adapt}} based on the scale variation ratio: ho{	ext{adapt}} = 	ext{clip}\left(  rac{st^2}{\hat{
u}k^2}, ho{\min}, ho{\max} ight) 3. Determine Adaptive Scale \sigma{	ext{adapt}}: Apply the masking operation M to determine the final adaptive dilation scale. If the current scale st is above the threshold ho{	ext{thre}} \cdot \sigmas, use the adaptive ratio; otherwise, use the minimal scale \epsilon to prevent invisible Gaussians from becoming visible: \sigma{	ext{adapt}} =  egin{cases} ho{	ext{adapt}} \cdot \sigmas & 	ext{if } st \ge ho{	ext{thre}} \cdot \sigmas \ \epsilon \cdot \sigmas & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} This defines the masking operation M (Eq. 12). 4. Apply Filter to Covariance: Update the covariance matrix \mathbf{\Sigma}k(t) by adding the adaptive dilation term \sigma{	ext{adapt}}^2 \mathbf{I} to the exponent: \mathbf{\Sigma}'k(t) = \mathbf{\Sigma}k(t) + \sigma{	ext{adapt}}^2 \mathbf{I} Scale the amplitude by \sqrt{|\mathbf{\Sigma}k(t)| / |\mathbf{\Sigma}k(t) + \sigma{	ext{adapt}}^2 \mathbf{I}|} to prevent distortion of the scale ratio between dimensions and avoid dilation when the primitive is invisible. 5. Integrate Masking into Splatting: Integrate the masking operation M into the splatting pipeline to avoid rendering invisible Gaussians. Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def identity(n): """Returns an n x n identity matrix.""" return [[1 if i == j else 0 for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)] def det(M): """Computes the determinant of a square matrix M recursively.""" n = len(M) if n == 1: return M[0][0] if n == 2: return M[0][0] M[1][1] - M[0][1] M[1][0] return sum((-1) i M[0][i] det([row[:i] + row[i+1:] for row in M[1:]]) for i in range(n)) def apply4dscaleadaptivefilter(gaussiank, timet, params): """ Applies the 4D Scale-adaptive Filter to a single 4D Gaussian primitive. Args: gaussiank: Object containing pk, Sigmak, st (current scale). timet: Current time step. params: Dictionary containing rhomin, rhomax, rhothre, sigmas, epsilon, and estimated frequency nuhatk. Returns: filteredsigma: Filtered covariance matrix. amplitudescale: Scaling factor for opacity/color. isvisible: Boolean mask indicating if the Gaussian should be splatted. """ # 1. Get parameters rhomin = params['rhomin'] rhomax = params['rhomax'] rhothre = params['rhothre'] sigmas = params['sigmas'] epsilon = params['epsilon'] nuhatk = params['nuhatk'] st = gaussiank.st Sigmak = gaussiank.Sigmak # Guard against division by zero in frequency estimate if nuhatk == 0: nuhatk = 1e-6 # 2. Compute adaptive dilation ratio rhoadapt ratio = (st 2) / (nuhatk 2) rhoadapt = max(rhomin, min(rhomax, ratio)) # 3. Determine adaptive scale sigmaadapt based on threshold thresholdval = rhothre sigmas if st >= thresholdval: sigmaadapt = rhoadapt sigmas isvisible = True else: sigmaadapt = epsilon sigmas isvisible = False # 4. Apply filter to covariance I4 = identity(4) Sigmafiltered = [[Sigmak[i][j] + (sigmaadapt 2) I4[i][j] for j in range(4)] for i in range(4)] detoriginal = det(Sigmak) detfiltered = det(Sigmafiltered) # Guard against numerical instability for degenerate Gaussians if detoriginal <= 0 or detfiltered <= 0: amplitudescale = 1.0 else: amplitudescale = (detoriginal / detfiltered) 0.5 return Sigmafiltered, amplitudescale, isvisible Precision: Determinant calculations for 4D matrices can suffer from numerical instability if the Gaussian is highly anisotropic or degenerate. Guard against negative determinants due to floating-point errors. Threshold Sensitivity: The parameter ho{	ext{thre}} is critical. A small value reduces sensitivity to lighting but may allow unwanted dilation; a large value may overly constrain the Gaussian. Invisible Gaussians: Without the masking operation M, dilation could make imperceptible Gaussians visible, introducing noise. The \epsilon term prevents this. Anisotropy Distortion: Fixed-scale dilation distorts the scale ratio between dimensions. The adaptive scaling ho{	ext{adapt}} preserves the dimensional scale ratio. Time Complexity: The filter adds a constant-time operation per Gaussian per frame (matrix addition, determinant calculation, clipping). It does not increase the asymptotic complexity of the splatting pipeline, which is dominated by rasterization. Memory Complexity: No additional memory is required beyond the existing Gaussian attributes. The frequency estimate \hat{
u}k requires one scalar per primitive. Performance: The filter improves anti-aliasing performance, particularly in monocular video reconstruction tasks, outperforming Mip-Splatting at higher resolutions when combined with scale regularization. It reduces unnecessary computation by masking invisible Gaussians. Strengths: Preserves Gaussian anisotropy; prevents dilation of invisible primitives; mitigates aliasing artifacts from scale variations. Limitations: Sensitive to the hyperparameter ho{	ext{thre}}. Requires accurate estimation of \hat{
u}k, which depends on camera parameters and deformation field stability. The 4D Scale-adaptive Filter was introduced in the context of 4D Gaussian Splatting to address artifacts caused by fixed-scale dilation in dynamic scenes. It builds on the principles of 2D Gaussian Splatting [6068-2d-gaussian-splatting.md] but extends them to 4D space. The method was proposed to overcome limitations of 3D smoothing filters, which do not account for visibility or anisotropy preservation in 4D. Alias-free 4D Gaussian Splatting, https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18367v1, fetched 2026-10-26 (unofficial)

This method assigns a learnable unit normal to each 3D Gaussian to replace noisy gradient-based estimates, preventing the optimization from overfitting to training lighting conditions by aligning rendered normals with geometry priors derived from expected depth gradients. It differs from pure gradient-based approaches by explicitly optimizing normal attributes per Gaussian and supervising them against pseudo-normals computed from expected depth. Inputs and Outputs: Input: 3D Gaussian parameters (position, covariance, opacity \alpha, view-dependent color), camera parameters, ground truth RGB images, and binary image masks M indicating valid pixels. Output: Optimized Gaussian normals \mathbf{n}i, rendered normal maps, and the normal loss value. Data Structures: Gaussian Normal: Each Gaussian i stores a learnable unit normal vector \mathbf{n}i \in \mathbb{R}^3. Rendered Normal: A 2D image buffer where each pixel stores a 3D vector \mathbf{n}i (pre-normalization) or the final normalized normal. Steps: 1. Compute Expected Depth: For each pixel, compute the expected depth zE along the ray using the weighted sum of Gaussian depths zi: zE =  rac{\sum{i \in N} wi zi}{\sum{i \in N} wi} where wi = Ti gi \alphai are the splatting weights, Ti is transmittance, gi is the Gaussian density, and \alphai is opacity. N is the set of Gaussians overlapping the pixel. 2. Compute Pseudo-Normals: Calculate the image-space gradients of the expected depth 
abla zE = (\partialx zE, \partialy zE). Convert these gradients into world-space pseudo-normals \hat{\mathbf{n}}
abla (or \mathbf{n}'i) using the camera projection matrix. 3. Splat Learnable Normals: For each pixel, accumulate the learnable normals \mathbf{n}i from overlapping Gaussians using the splatting weights wi: 	ilde{\mathbf{n}} =  rac{\sum{i \in N} wi \mathbf{n}i}{\sum{i \in N} wi + \epsilonn} where \epsilonn is a small constant for numerical stability. 4. Normalize Rendered Normals: L2-normalize the accumulated normal 	ilde{\mathbf{n}} to ensure unit length, as alpha-blending does not preserve unit length: \hat{\mathbf{n}} =  rac{	ilde{\mathbf{n}}}{\|	ilde{\mathbf{n}}\|2 + \epsilonn} 5. Compute Loss: Calculate the normal loss Ln as the sum of the pseudo-normal alignment loss and a total variation smoothness term: L{n-p} = \|\hat{\mathbf{n}} - \hat{\mathbf{n}}
abla\| Ln = L{n-p} + \lambda{n-TV} TV{normal} Alternatively, as described in, the loss can be formulated as minimizing the negative dot product (maximizing cosine similarity) between the normalized rendered normal and the pseudo-normal: L{normal} = -\sum{i \in M} \left(  rac{\mathbf{n}i}{\|\mathbf{n}i\|} \cdot \mathbf{n}'i ight) where M is the binary image mask selecting valid pixels. The sources present slightly different formulations for the alignment term (L{n-p} vs L{normal}), but both aim to align the rendered normal with the depth-derived pseudo-normal. Parameters: \epsilonn: Small constant for numerical stability in normalization denominator. Typical value: 10^{-6}. \lambda{n-TV}: Weight for the total variation loss. Not given in the sources. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def computenormalloss(gaussians, camera, mask, epsilonn=1e-6, lambdantv=0.1): H, W = camera.height, camera.width expecteddepth = [[0.0] W for in range(H)] accnormal = [[[0.0, 0.0, 0.0] for in range(W)] for in range(H)] accweight = [[0.0] W for in range(H)] # Rasterization step to find overlapping Gaussians per pixel for y in range(H): for x in range(W): for g in gaussiansoverlapping(x, y): w = g.transmittance g.density g.opacity expecteddepth[y][x] += w g.depth accnormal[y][x][0] += w g.normal[0] accnormal[y][x][1] += w g.normal[1] accnormal[y][x][2] += w g.normal[2] accweight[y][x] += w # Compute pseudo-normals from expected depth gradients depthgradx = [[expecteddepth[y][min(x+1, W-1)] - expecteddepth[y][x] for x in range(W)] for y in range(H)] depthgrady = [[expecteddepth[min(y+1, H-1)][x] - expecteddepth[y][x] for x in range(W)] for y in range(H)] pseudonormals = [[None] W for in range(H)] for y in range(H): for x in range(W): # World-space normal from depth gradient: n = (-dz/dx, -dz/dy, 1) n = [-depthgradx[y][x], -depthgrady[y][x], 1.0] norm = sqrt(n[0]2 + n[1]2 + n[2]2) pseudonormals[y][x] = [v / norm for v in n] # Transform pseudo-normals to world space if needed # Normalize accumulated learnable normals normalizednormals = [[[0.0, 0.0, 0.0] for in range(W)] for in range(H)] for y in range(H): for x in range(W): norm = sqrt(accnormal[y][x][0]2 + accnormal[y][x][1]2 + accnormal[y][x][2]2) normalizednormals[y][x] = [accnormal[y][x][i] / (norm + epsilonn) for i in range(3)] # Compute L2 alignment loss (S2) or dot-product loss (S1) lossnormal = 0.0 for y in range(H): for x in range(W): if mask[y][x] > 0.5: diff = [normalizednormals[y][x][i] - pseudonormals[y][x][i] for i in range(3)] lossnormal += sqrt(diff[0]2 + diff[1]2 + diff[2]2) # Compute Total Variation loss tvloss = 0.0 for y in range(H): for x in range(W): for i in range(3): if x < W - 1: tvloss += abs(normalizednormals[y][x+1][i] - normalizednormals[y][x][i]) if y < H - 1: tvloss += abs(normalizednormals[y+1][x][i] - normalizednormals[y][x][i]) return lossnormal + lambdantv tvloss Edge Cases and Pitfalls: Precision: The normalization denominator must include a small epsilon to avoid division by zero. Degenerate Input: If a pixel has no overlapping Gaussians, the accumulated normal is zero, leading to undefined normalization. The mask M selects valid pixels for the loss summation. The parent method that uses depth derivatives to supervise the normal field [9627-normal-field-supervision-via-rendering-and-depth-derivatives.md]. This method extends it by introducing learnable normals per Gaussian. The base rendering technique that this method extends with normal attributes [52-gaussian-splatting-volume-rendering.md]. Time Complexity: Additional overhead per pixel for accumulating normals and computing depth gradients. The depth gradient computation is O(HW), and the normal accumulation is part of the standard rasterization pass. Memory Complexity: Requires storing an additional learnable normal vector per Gaussian and rendering buffers for expected depth and accumulated norm