Gaussian Scale Regularization is a constraint term used during the optimization of 3D Gaussian Splatting to prevent the formation of excessively large Gaussians, which can degrade rendering quality and stability. It specifically addresses issues arising from large input triangles or the unconstrained training of Gaussian scales by forcing the maximum scale of a Gaussian to remain within a threshold of the circumradius of its associated mesh triangle. Unlike general isotropic regularization which merely encourages equal scaling in all directions, this method ties the scale limit directly to the geometric resolution of the underlying mesh. Inputs and Outputs: Input: A set of NG Gaussians, where each Gaussian i has a scale vector s^i (three components representing scales along principal axes) and is associated with a specific mesh triangle. The mesh triangle is defined by vertices, from which edge vectors are derived. Output: A scalar loss value L{sc} representing the regularization penalty. Data Structures: Gaussian Scale (s^i): A vector of three floating-point values. In standard 3DGS, these are often stored as logarithms of the scales for optimization stability, but the loss operates on the exponential (actual) scale values. Circumradius (r^{i}{sc}): A single floating-point value representing the radius of the circle passing through the three vertices of the triangle associated with Gaussian i. Formulas: The circumradius rc of a triangle is computed from two edge vectors a and b sharing a common vertex: r{c} =  rac {
