orm{\cdot} denotes the Euclidean norm (L2 norm) and 	imes denotes the cross product. The regularization loss L{sc} is defined as: L{sc} = \sum{i \in NG} \max(\max(s^i) - \kappa{rc} r^{i}{sc}, 0) where: NG is the total number of Gaussians. s^i is the scale vector for Gaussian i. \max(s^i) is the largest component of the scale vector s^i. r^{i}{sc} is the circumradius of the triangle associated with Gaussian i. \kappa{rc} is a constant scale factor. Parameters: \kappa{rc}: The threshold constant. Empirically set to 0.2. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def gaussianscaleregularization(gaussians, meshtriangles, kapparc=0.2): """ Computes the Gaussian Scale Regularization loss. Args: gaussians: List or array of Gaussian objects/structures. Each element i has a 'scales' attribute (array of 3 floats). Each element i has a 'triangleindex' attribute mapping to meshtriangles. meshtriangles: List or array of triangle definitions. Each element j is defined by 3 vertices (v0, v1, v2). Each vertex is a 3D vector (x, y, z). kapparc: Float, the scale factor (default 0.2). Returns: loss: Float, the total regularization loss. """ totalloss = 0.0 for i in range(len(gaussians)): # Get the scale vector for the i-th Gaussian # Note: If scales are stored as logs, exponentiate them here. si = gaussians[i].scales # Find the maximum scale in the three directions maxscale = max(si[0], si[1], si[2]) # Get the associated triangle triidx = gaussians[i].triangleindex v0, v1, v2 = meshtriangles[triidx].vertices # Compute edge vectors sharing a vertex (e.g., v0) a = v1 - v0 b = v2 - v0 # Compute circumradius rc # norm(a) is sqrt(a.x^2 + a.y^2 + a.z^2) norma = sqrt(dot(a, a)) normb = sqrt(dot(b, b)) normab = sqrt(dot(a - b, a - b)) # Cross product a x b crossab = cross(a, b) normcrossab = sqrt(dot(crossab, crossab)) # Avoid division by zero if triangle is degenerate if normcrossab < 1